WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.266
Crypto misuse usually comes from using a reasonable-looking tool for the wrong goal, using an approved library incorrectly, or ignoring a failure

2
00:00:09.266 --> 00:00:11.280
result that should stop processing.

3
00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:17.726
Common review findings include encryption without authentication, nonce reuse, weak randomness, accepting unauthenticated data, custom trust

4
00:00:17.726 --> 00:00:22.157
decisions, logging keys, hardcoded secrets, stale libraries, and inconsistent error handling.

5
00:00:22.157 --> 00:00:25.783
Testing should verify application behavior, not reprove the mathematics.

6
00:00:25.783 --> 00:00:35.049
Use known-good library behavior, then test that the application passes the right inputs, checks return values, rejects invalid or corrupted data, and

7
00:00:35.049 --> 00:00:37.466
does not continue after verification fails.

8
00:00:37.466 --> 00:00:39.480
Failure paths need deliberate coverage.

9
00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:45.926
Corrupted ciphertext, wrong associated data, invalid signatures, expired certificates, missing keys, unavailable random sources, and configuration

10
00:00:45.926 --> 00:00:49.149
mistakes should all lead to predictable, safe outcomes.

11
00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:57.609
Code review should identify key ownership, nonce policy, algorithm or mode selection, trust anchors, library return-value handling, and the boundary

12
00:00:57.609 --> 00:01:00.429
where data becomes trusted enough to process.

13
00:01:00.429 --> 00:01:03.249
Dependency maintenance is part of crypto safety.

14
00:01:03.249 --> 00:01:08.486
Track library versions, supported algorithms, deprecation guidance, platform updates, and organizational policy changes.

15
00:01:08.486 --> 00:01:11.709
A once-acceptable configuration may need revision later.

16
00:01:11.709 --> 00:01:16.140
Crypto code should be small enough that these checks are practical.

17
00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:24.197
If the logic is spread across many layers or hidden behind vague wrappers, important trust decisions become harder to review.

18
00:01:24.197 --> 00:01:27.017
Review should also include configuration and deployment.

19
00:01:27.017 --> 00:01:36.686
The code may call the right API, but a weak key source, disabled validation mode, stale dependency, or permissive runtime setting can still weaken

20
00:01:36.686 --> 00:01:37.491
the boundary.

21
00:01:37.491 --> 00:01:40.311
Good tests assert that failures stay failures.

22
00:01:40.311 --> 00:01:47.563
They should verify that the application does not quietly fall back to unprotected behavior when cryptographic operations fail.

23
00:01:47.563 --> 00:01:52.800
That makes misuse visible during ordinary engineering work instead of during production recovery.
